{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>לֹא יִמָּצֵא בְּבָתֵּיכֶם</b> — From where do we derive the borders? Scripture says: \"בְּכָל גְּבוּלֶךָ\" (Exodus 13:7). What then does \"בְּבָתֵּיכֶם\" teach? Just as your house is in your domain, so too your border is in your domain. This excludes the leaven of a gentile that is with an Israelite who did not accept responsibility for it — this is Rashi's explanation (Rashi on Exodus 12:19). And it is not correct in my view, for there is nothing in the implication of \"in your domain\" to exclude the leaven of a gentile, for it is in his domain, and houses and borders are equivalent in this regard, for both are his domain while the property belongs to others. Furthermore, leaven of a gentile for which he did not accept responsibility does not fit into this analogy, for the simple sense of houses is no more lenient than that of borders; rather, in houses, the prohibition is implied from this very verse, which is written \"לֹא יִמָּצֵא,\" and its permissibility is derived for us from what is written (Exodus 13:7) \"לֹא יֵרָאֶה לְךָ שְׂאֹר\" — \"yours\": you may not see, but you may see that of others and that of the Most High. And I have only derived this for the borders; from where do I derive it for houses? It is learned from the verbal analogy of שְׂאֹר שְׂאֹר (leaven, leaven), as is explained at the beginning of tractate Pesachim (5a).\n\nBut this midrash that they expounded — \"just as your house is in your domain, so too your border is in your domain\" — serves only to exclude the leaven of an Israelite that is in the domain of a gentile. And so it is stated in the Mekhilta (here): \"'בְּבָתֵּיכֶם' — why is this said? Since it is stated 'בְּכָל גְּבוּלֶךָ,' I might have understood it literally; therefore Scripture says 'בְּבָתֵּיכֶם.' Just as your house is in your domain, so too your border is in your domain. This excludes the leaven of an Israelite that is in the domain of a gentile — even though he can remove it, it is not in his domain; this excludes the leaven of a gentile that is in the domain of an Israelite, and leaven upon which a collapse has fallen — even though it is in his domain, he cannot remove it,\" etc. And its meaning is that it might have been understood from \"וְלֹא יֵרָאֶה לְךָ בְּכָל גְּבוּלֶךָ\" that we may not leave our leaven anywhere in our borders, even in the house of a gentile; therefore \"בְּבָתֵּיכֶם\" comes to exclude the house of a gentile.\n\nAnd we learn from this baraita that we are not warned by the Torah except that we must not keep our leaven in our domain, whether in our houses or in our borders. But if we deposited it in the hands of a gentile in his house, we do not transgress on account of בַּל יֵרָאֶה (it shall not be seen) and בַּל יִמָּצֵא (it shall not be found). And this is indeed the case, for if you do not say so, then for his own leaven he would transgress everywhere, even if he deposited it with a gentile in a land across the sea, while for the leaven of a gentile he would not transgress even in the house of an Israelite. If so, why is \"בְּבָתֵּיכֶם\" said, and why is \"בְּגְבוּלֶךָ\" said? There is no distinction between our houses and borders and the houses and borders of gentiles — rather, we are not warned at all by the Torah except regarding our domain. But by rabbinic decree, we are required to remove our leaven from every place.\n\nAccordingly, they said in the Gemara (Pesachim 6a): \"If he designated a house for it, he does not transgress on account of it\" — meaning that leaven of a gentile for which he accepted responsibility, even though it is judged as his own, is prohibited only in the domain of the Israelite, but in the domain of a gentile it is permitted, and even by rabbinic decree they did not decree against it at all. And the Rav did not hold this view in his explanation of the Gemara in Pesachim. And what they concluded in the baraita that we cited — \"this excludes the leaven of a gentile that is in the domain of an Israelite\" — this is derived from the analogy between houses and borders: just as in the borders it is permitted, as it is written \"לְךָ\" — \"yours\": you may not see, but you may see that of others and that of the Most High — so too in houses it is permitted. And it also excludes leaven upon which a great collapse has fallen, such that he cannot clear the pile, and it is lost to him and to all men, which is permitted, for it is not called \"yours.\""], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "I read מפולת גדולה in the final sentence as 'a great collapse' (i.e., a landslide or ruin), consistent with the baraita cited earlier; the last clause לא קרינא ביה שלך means 'it is not called yours.' The phrase 'that of the Most High' (של גבוה) refers to property of the Temple (hekdesh)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:06:35.486764308Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}